  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes three developmental roles of Notch signaling by comparing normal tissue with tissue lacking Notch activity. In the wild-type condition, Notch helps neighboring cells adopt different fates rather than all becoming the same. One example is lateral inhibition, where initially similar cells resolve into a mixed pattern with scattered distinct cells surrounded by alternative cell types. Another is lineage choice: a branching cell-family diagram suggests that Notch influences how progenitor cells diversify into several different descendant fates, whereas without Notch the lineage appears simplified and biased toward one outcome. \n\nThe third example shows inductive signaling in a layered tissue. In the normal case, signaling between adjacent rows of cells seems to generate a specific band of differentiated cells at a boundary, while the absence of Notch removes or disrupts that induced stripe. Across all three cartoons, the main message is that Notch is a local cell–cell communication pathway that creates spatial pattern, promotes fate diversification, and organizes boundaries within developing tissues. Without it, cells appear more uniform, less diverse, and less precisely arranged.",
